{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/9/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b2bc3ffd\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nMove every object above the solid bottom stripe upward by its own pixel count.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background, exactly one full-width non-background row\nthat acts as a fixed stripe, and one or more 4-connected monochrome objects\nabove that stripe.\n\nRule\nClear each object from its original cells, then translate it straight upward\nby the number of pixels in that object, preserving its shape and color. Leave\nthe full-width stripe unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nEach object's area is also its vertical displacement; the full-width row is a\nfixed baseline, not a moving object.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9caf5b84\" lines=\"28-45\">\n<description>\nRecolor every pixel whose color is not one of the two most frequent input colors to orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid contains at least three colors.\n- Orange is absent from the input and is used only as the output replacement color.\n- Exactly two colors occupy the top two frequency ranks; they may tie with each\n  other, but no third color ties them at the cutoff.\n\nRule:\n- Preserve every pixel whose color is one of the two most frequent input colors.\n- Replace every other pixel with orange.\n\nKey insight:\n- The visible distinction is not the numeric identity of the colors but their\n  population: the two majority colors remain, while all minority colors merge\n  into the new orange color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e048c9ed\" lines=\"11-26\">\n<description>\nAdd a quantity-color label in the top marker column for each horizontal bar.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a black background and one non-background marker pixel in the top row.\n- Below the marker row, every non-empty source row contains one solid monochrome horizontal bar to the left of the marker column.\n- Source rows are staggered every other row.\n\nRule\n- Copy the input grid.\n- For each source bar, read its quantity as the bar length.\n- Paint the quantity color in the marker column on the same row as the bar:\n  2->1, 3->4, 4->9, 5->6.\n\nKey insight\n- The grey marker supplies the result column, while each bar supplies a small quantity with a fixed color code.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"4a1cacc2\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nFill the rectangle from the lone foreground pixel to its nearest grid corner.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a uniform background field with exactly one foreground pixel.\n- The foreground pixel's color is the color used for all added pixels.\n\nRule\n- Find the grid corner with the smallest Manhattan distance to the foreground pixel.\n- Recolor every pixel in the inclusive axis-aligned rectangle bounded by that\n  corner and the foreground pixel to the foreground color.\n\nKey insight\n- The single pixel is one corner of the rectangle; the opposite corner is the\n  nearest corner of the whole canvas.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f0f8a26d\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nRotate every foreground bar 90 degrees around its own center on the same canvas.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color, and all foreground pixels share one color.\nEach 4-connected foreground object is a solid one-cell-thick horizontal or vertical\nbar with odd length, with single-pixel bars allowed.\n\nRule\nClear the canvas to the background, then redraw every bar with its midpoint, length,\nand color preserved while swapping horizontal and vertical orientation.\n\nKey insight\nThe bars are independent objects: their centers and lengths identify the output\nplacement, so no matching, ordering, or global symmetry is needed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"195c6913\" lines=\"37-52\">\n<description>\nSimulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).\n- Isolated single pixels of a cycle color: start markers adjacent to the path.\n- A 2x2 monochrome block of a distinct color: end marker.\n\nRule:\n- Each start marker launches a particle moving straight, painting cycle colors in order.\n- On hitting a boundary, paint that cell with the end color and turn 90°.\n- Turn direction alternates: odd collisions → left (CCW), even → right (CW).\n- If the scheduled turn is blocked, the particle stops.\n\nKey insight: The alternating left/right turn rule is the core mechanism; without it the path would be trivial.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9aaea919\" lines=\"38-51\">\n<description>\nTransfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.\n- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.\n- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.\n- All marker bars are removed.\n\nKey insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c6141b15\" lines=\"84-95\">\n<description>\nSwap a connector segment with repeated marker sprites by connecting the marker anchors and stamping the sprite at the segment endpoints.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors. One foreground color forms a single straight 8-connected segment. The other foreground color forms two or more identical centered sprites.\n\nRule\nBuild a background canvas of the same size. Draw straight connector-color segments between every pair of original sprite anchors. Then stamp the marker sprite, using its centered anchor, onto the two endpoints of the original connector segment.\n\nKey insight\nThe original segment endpoints are the destinations for the repeated sprite, while the original sprite centers become the vertices of the new connector-color complete graph.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b7256dcd\" lines=\"18-32\">\n<description>\nTransfer each colored marker to its adjacent neutral mask component, then erase the markers.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the most common color.\n- The neutral mask color is the unique most frequent foreground color.\n- Other foreground colors are marker pixels that may touch neutral 4-connected mask components.\nRule\n- Remove every marker pixel by recoloring it to the background.\n- For each 4-connected neutral mask component, find marker colors touching it by edge adjacency.\n- If a component touches one marker color, recolor the whole neutral component to that marker color.\n- If a component touches no marker color, leave it neutral.\nKey insight\n- Marker pixels label neighboring neutral regions but are not part of the final colored objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fbf15a0b\" lines=\"49-69\">\n<description>\nCrop out the grid half containing the two aligned marker pixels, then erase the markers to background.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one non-background marker color\n  that appears as exactly two cells.\n- The marker cells are aligned either in one row or one column and both lie\n  in the same half along that direction.\n- Other non-background pixels are content inside one or both halves.\n\nRule\n- If the markers share a row, keep the left or right half whose columns\n  contain them.\n- If the markers share a column, keep the top or bottom half whose rows\n  contain them.\n- In the kept half, recolor the marker pixels to the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The two marker pixels are not part of the picture; their alignment and\n  half-position identify which half of the doubled canvas is the answer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8f52ecc\" lines=\"107-122\">\n<description>\nConnect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.\n- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.\n\nRule\n- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.\n- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.\n- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
